\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for NER.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for NER, output convergence (degeneracy $12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking---a \textit{capability-diversity tension} observed across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}) and scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
A free-form decoding ablation confirms degeneracy is model-intrinsic: CoNLL free-form degeneracy is $59.3$\% versus $56.7$\% constrained ($\Delta{=}2.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:freeform_ablation}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The cascade provides a pre-deployment diagnostic: measure degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment with $N{=}8$ samples before investing in $N{\times}$ inference.
When degeneracy exceeds $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, aggregation is unlikely to help; when both conditions are favorable ($D{<}22\%$, LP $\rho{>}0.2$), entity-level construction offers modest gains.
Cascade thresholds (degeneracy ${>}30\%$, $\alpha{<}0.05$) are empirically stable across our four NER datasets but may require task-specific calibration; SciERC conclusions throughout are tentative ($n{=}529$, single training seed).
These findings are validated on 7--14B fine-tuned NER; whether the cascade generalizes to other structured tasks---broader IE, parsing, code generation---remains an important open question requiring dedicated investigation.

\paragraph{Conclusion.}
We characterize when test-time compute scaling fails for NER, with exploratory extensions to RE and SQL.
The diagnostic cascade---identifying output convergence and signal alignment collapse as boundary conditions---is our primary methodological contribution.
The capability-diversity tension likely extends beyond NER to other structured output tasks, but this remains a hypothesis requiring empirical validation.

\section{Related Work}\label{sec:related}

\paragraph{Test-time compute scaling.}
Self-consistency \cite{wang2023selfconsistency} and best-of-$N$ \cite{snell2025scaling, brown2024monkeys} drive large gains on reasoning and code \cite{chen2021codex}.
Reward-free selectors \cite{taubenfeld2025cisc, kang2025selfcertainty}, BoN scaling laws \cite{gao2023scaling}, and trained verifiers \cite{cobbe2021verifiers, lightman2024verify} target discrete-answer settings; NER requires different aggregation because degeneracy operates at the entity-set level.

\paragraph{SC failure modes.}
\citet{chen2023twofailures} identify SC failures where consistent outputs are wrong; free-form \cite{chen2024usc} and atomic \cite{thirukovalluru2024atomic} SC variants face limits where sub-output agreement does not yield selection gain (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).

\paragraph{Consistency for structured IE.}
\citet{yao2026consistency} show task-level QE correlation ($\rho$ up to 0.47) for NER/RE; \citet{xie2023zeroshot} propose entity voting for zero-shot NER and \citet{xie2024selfimproving} report positive SC where zero-shot diversity is high.
\paragraph{Uncertainty estimation and MBR.}
Our selection instantiates MBR decoding \cite{eikema2022sampling, denero2009fast}; despite success in translation \cite{freitag2022high} and code \cite{jinnai2024mbr}, all variants fail due to instance degeneracy (Appendix~\ref{app:mbr_connection}).
\citet{eikema2025structured} find MBR utilities degrade on structured outputs; our cascade localizes this to degeneracy and LP compression (\S\ref{sec:selection_gap}).
LP is well-calibrated at population level (ECE $0.069$--$0.096$) but too compressed within instances for selection \cite{kadavath2022language, tian2023calibration}.

\paragraph{Capability ceiling vs.\ diversity collapse.}\label{sec:yun_contrast}
\citet{yun2025price} identify \textit{diversity collapse} under constrained decoding for discrete answers (Table~\ref{tab:differentiation}).
Our degeneracy differs definitionally: they measure response-level diversity loss in discrete answers; we measure entity-set convergence where identical outputs eliminate selection signal.
The four-stage cascade provides quantitative diagnostics (degeneracy rate, $\alpha$, LP compression) absent from their phenomenological account.

\paragraph{Complementary approaches.}
Conformal prediction provides structured output coverage guarantees but requires exchangeability assumptions violated by degeneracy; token-level uncertainty decomposition operates below entity level; entity-level MBR with F1 utility is conceptually our entity construction, formalized in \S\ref{sec:mbr_theory}.

\paragraph{What is new.}
We provide the first instance-level diagnostic cascade for NER: \citet{yao2026consistency} report task-level correlations; \citet{chen2023twofailures} identify failure modes without quantitative boundary characterization; \citet{xie2023zeroshot} propose entity voting without boundary analysis; \citet{yun2025price} study discrete-answer diversity.

\section{Discussion and Conclusion}\label{sec:discussion}\label{sec:selection_method}\label{sec:conclusion}

\paragraph{Why test-time scaling fails for NER.}
For discrete-answer reasoning, sample diversity reflects diverse reasoning paths; for NER, output convergence (degeneracy $12$--$68$\%) eliminates candidate diversity while signal alignment collapse ($\alpha{\to}0$) prevents ranking---a \textit{capability-diversity tension} observed across model families (5.5--74\%; Table~\ref{tab:frontier_sc_cross}) and scales (4B--72B; \S\ref{sec:scaling}).
A free-form decoding ablation confirms degeneracy is model-intrinsic: CoNLL free-form degeneracy is $59.3$\% versus $56.7$\% constrained ($\Delta{=}2.6$\,pp; Appendix~\ref{app:freeform_ablation}).

\paragraph{Practical implications.}
The cascade provides a pre-deployment diagnostic: measure degeneracy and LP-F1 alignment with $N{=}8$ samples before investing in $N{\times}$ inference; when degeneracy exceeds $\theta_{\text{crit}}{=}0.70$, aggregation is unlikely to help.
Cascade thresholds are empirically stable across our NER datasets but may require task-specific calibration; SciERC conclusions are tentative ($n{=}529$, single seed).
These findings are validated on 7--14B fine-tuned NER; generalization to broader IE, parsing, and code generation requires dedicated investigation.

